So this is my step to deploy a new alpha version:

```yaml
      - name: Deploy iOS alpha to TestFlight
        env:
          # The following secrets are used by the Codemagic CLI tool. It's important
          # to use the same names as the CLI tool expects.
          CERTIFICATE_PRIVATE_KEY: ${{ secrets.SHAREZONE_CERTIFICATE_PRIVATE_KEY }}
          APP_STORE_CONNECT_KEY_IDENTIFIER: ${{ secrets.SHAREZONE_APP_STORE_CONNECT_KEY_IDENTIFIER }}
          APP_STORE_CONNECT_ISSUER_ID: ${{ secrets.SHAREZONE_APP_STORE_CONNECT_ISSUER_ID }}
          APP_STORE_CONNECT_PRIVATE_KEY: ${{ secrets.SHAREZONE_APP_STORE_CONNECT_PRIVATE_KEY }}
        run: |
          # Because we are publishing every commit a new alpha version, we are
          # able to use the last commit message (title and description) as release
          # note for the alpha builds. This is not the most user friendly note but
          # it's better than nothing.
          #
          # The "sed 's/[<>]//g'" part is to remove the "<" and ">" characters
          # because the App Store doesn't allow them.
          export LAST_COMMIT_MESSAGE=$(git log -1 --pretty=%B | sed 's/[<>]//g')

          # The App Store Connect API only allows a maximum of 4000 characters for
          # the release notes. So we need to truncate the commit message if it's
          # longer than 4000 characters.
          export SHORT_LAST_COMMIT_MESSAGE=${LAST_COMMIT_MESSAGE:0:4000}

          # Tempoary prints
          echo "LAST_COMMIT_MESSAGE: $LAST_COMMIT_MESSAGE"
          echo "SHORT_LAST_COMMIT_MESSAGE: $SHORT_LAST_COMMIT_MESSAGE"

          sz deploy ios \
            --stage alpha \
            --whats-new="$SHORT_LAST_COMMIT_MESSAGE" \
            --export-options-plist=$HOME/export_options.plist
```

As you can see, I pass the last commit message as "whats-new" parameter. The `sz deploy ios` command is my own CLI that I programmed written in Dart. This is the relevant code for the CLI:

```dart
class DeployIosCommand extends Command {
  final SharezoneRepo _repo;

  DeployIosCommand(this._repo) {
    argParser
      ..addOption(
        releaseStageOptionName,
        abbr: 's',
        allowed: _iosStages,
        help:
            'The deployment stage to deploy to. The "stable" stage is used for App Store releases, the "alpha" stage is used for TestFlight releases. The value will be forwarded to the "sz build" command.',
        defaultsTo: 'stable',
      )
      ..addOption(
        exportOptionsPlistName,
        help:
            'Export an IPA with these options. See "xcodebuild -h" for available exportOptionsPlist keys.',
      )
      ..addOption(
        flavorOptionName,
        allowed: _iosFlavors,
        help: 'The flavor to build for. Only the "prod" flavor is supported.',
        defaultsTo: 'prod',
      );

    addAppStoreConnectKeyIdOption(argParser);
    addAppStoreConnectIssuerIdOption(argParser);
    addAppStoreConnectPrivateKey(argParser);
    addCertificateKey(argParser);
    addWhatsNewOption(argParser);
  }

  static const flavorOptionName = 'flavor';
  static const exportOptionsPlistName = 'export-options-plist';

  List<String> get _iosStages => _iosStageToTracks.keys.toList();

  @override
  String get description =>
      'Deploys the Sharezone iOS app to the App Store or TestFlight. Automatically bumps the build number and builds the app. Codemagic CLI tools are required.';

  @override
  String get name => 'ios';

  @override
  Future<void> run() async {
    _throwIfFlavorIsNotSupportForDeployment();
    await throwIfCodemagicCliToolsAreNotInstalled();

    final whatsNew = argResults![whatsNewOptionName] as String?;
    stdout.writeln('Deploy iOS with the following release notes:\n$whatsNew');
```

My problem is that the "--whats-new" command is null but I don't understand why. I can't reproduce it locally. On my main branch the logs are the following:

```
LAST_COMMIT_MESSAGE: Add temporary logs to understand why changelog is often missing for alpha versions (#975)

I tried it on a feature branch but it worked there. Therefore, let's try
it on the `main` branch.

Related to #922
SHORT_LAST_COMMIT_MESSAGE: Add temporary logs to understand why changelog is often missing for alpha versions (#975)

I tried it on a feature branch but it worked there. Therefore, let's try
it on the `main` branch.

Related to #922
Deploy iOS with the following release notes:
null
```

So you can see, the var "SHORT_LAST_COMMIT_MESSAGE" has the changelog message but when coming to the Dart and my print statement, the "whatsNew" var is null. Why?

Yes, it seems like you're facing an issue with how the shell handles multiline strings. You have a few options for how to proceed:

1. **Replace newline characters with spaces**: Before passing the `SHORT_LAST_COMMIT_MESSAGE` variable to the Dart script, you could replace all newline characters with spaces to make it a single-line string. You've already made this transformation to some extent in your shell script:

    ```yaml
    export LAST_COMMIT_MESSAGE=$(git log -1 --pretty=%B | sed 's/[<>]//g')
    export SHORT_LAST_COMMIT_MESSAGE=$(echo $LAST_COMMIT_MESSAGE | tr '\n' ' ')
    ```

2. **Use Base64 encoding and decoding**: You can encode the multiline string to a single-line Base64 string and decode it in your Dart script. Here’s how you can do it:

    In your GitHub Action:

    ```yaml
    export SHORT_LAST_COMMIT_MESSAGE=$(echo "$LAST_COMMIT_MESSAGE" | base64)
    ```

    And in your Dart script, decode it:

    ```dart
    import 'dart:convert';

    final whatsNew = argResults![whatsNewOptionName] as String?;
    if (whatsNew != null) {
      final decodedWhatsNew = utf8.decode(base64.decode(whatsNew));
      stdout.writeln('Deploy iOS with the following release notes:\n$decodedWhatsNew');
    }
    ```

3. **Pass the Message as a File**: If your release notes can be very large, you might hit shell command length limits. Another safe option is to write the message to a temporary file and then read it in your Dart program.

    In your GitHub Action:

    ```yaml
    echo "$LAST_COMMIT_MESSAGE" > temp_release_notes.txt
    ```

    Pass the filename to your Dart program:

    ```yaml
    sz deploy ios \
      --stage alpha \
      --whats-new=temp_release_notes.txt \
      --export-options-plist=$HOME/export_options.plist
    ```

    And in your Dart program, read the contents of the file:

    ```dart
    import 'dart:io';

    final whatsNew = argResults![whatsNewOptionName] as String?;
    if (whatsNew != null) {
      final file = File(whatsNew);
      if (await file.exists()) {
        final whatsNewContent = await file.readAsString();
        stdout.writeln('Deploy iOS with the following release notes:\n$whatsNewContent');
      } else {
        stdout.writeln('Deploy iOS with the following release notes:\n$whatsNew');
      }
    }
    ```

I recommend trying the first solution. If it doesn’t work, then you can try the second or third solution as a more robust fix. Let me know how it goes!

